  "Foxglow Cap": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A pearl-stemmed mushroom whose indigo cap carries nine faint points of foxfire and whose glassy gills brighten when the sanctuary's mycelial current passes beneath it. Cut flesh pulses like a tiny sleeping heartbeat; cooked, it tastes of cedar smoke and sweet chestnut, while a raw cap produces one night of vivid but non-prophetic dreams. Alchemists use it to stabilize restorative mixtures and to give safe light to living terrariums. Price: 5-15 cp.",
    "name": "Foxglow Cap"
  },
  "Lantern-Tree Sap": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Warm amber resin drawn only from a lantern tree's naturally opened seams. In a vial it separates into fine violet-gold ribbons that curl toward nearby living roots; brushed onto wood, paper, or stone, it forms a luminous lacquer that neither burns nor smokes and fades slowly over several days. Sanctuary artisans prize it because new coats bond cleanly to old ones without trapping rot beneath. Price: 10-25 cp.",
    "name": "Lantern-Tree Sap"
  },
  "Moonlace Bloom": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Consumable",
    "description": "A silver-blue meadow flower whose petals appear stitched together by threads of dew visible only in moonlight or rain. Steeped whole, it produces a cool floral tea that steadies trembling, softens ordinary fear, and settles restless sleep without changing judgment or erasing emotion; once spent, every luminous thread unravels into ordinary water. The best blossoms are gathered after duskbees finish pollinating them. Price: 15-40 cp.",
    "name": "Moonlace Bloom"
  },
  "Glowmoss Stone": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A smooth marsh pebble enclosed over nine dusks by layer after layer of luminous moss until plant and mineral become one cool, green-blue stone. It drinks daylight, foxfire, or spell-glow and releases that light evenly through the night; carved pieces retain a dim reserve even after years of use. Low Reaches keepers use uncut stones to mark stable footing without disturbing the wetland's darkness. Price: 20-50 cp.",
    "name": "Glowmoss Stone"
  },
  "Foxfire Dew Bead": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A dawn drop from Westreach grass crystallized around a mote of harmless foxfire. Each bead contains a tiny violet spiral and hums when strung beside others; cracking one releases several minutes of soft light and a scent of wet clover before the shell evaporates completely. Strings are used as trail markers, festival jewelry, and one-use signals that leave no glass behind. Price: 5-12 cp each, usually sold in strings of ten (50-120 cp).",
    "name": "Foxfire Dew Bead"
  },
  "Spirit Pollen": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Fine luminous pollen swept from the First Meadow only after its warm-snow fall has finished. Loosed into still air, the grains drift toward active magical residue and briefly outline a spell's physical wake, ward boundary, or recently used enchanted object without identifying its caster or revealing hidden history. Spent grains turn charcoal-grey and enrich ordinary garden soil. Price: 25-60 cp per pouch.",
    "name": "Spirit Pollen"
  },
  "Blueheart Ridge Chip": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A dark Rift March stone split by one cold cobalt vein, as though a piece of midnight retained a single pulse. The vein brightens in response to nearby magical or physical vibration—not private emotion—and can be tuned into a quiet lamp, fault alarm, or resonance marker. Fresh chips smell faintly of rain on iron and stay cold even inside a forge. Price: 30-80 cp.",
    "name": "Blueheart Ridge Chip"
  },
  "Stillmoss Peat": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A dense brick cut from dead growth beneath the Dim March's living moss, cured until it becomes lighter than dry wood. It burns with a steady pearl flame that gives light without heat, scent, smoke, or crackle, making it ideal for vigils, precision work, and chambers where ordinary fire would disturb stored materials. Ash from a finished brick is inert and welcomed back by the moss. Price: 10-20 cp per brick.",
    "name": "Stillmoss Peat"
  },
  "Saltgrass Chime": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A hollow white seed-node harvested after Quiet Shore saltgrass rings its one natural note. Touched again, it answers with a softer version of the first sound it heard, then falls silent forever; a strand therefore records a brief sequence of disturbances without naming what caused them. Moonplovers line their nests with spent nodes, and careful collectors always leave enough for the next grass generation. Price: 15-35 cp. A full strand (twelve grains): 1-2 sp.",
    "name": "Saltgrass Chime"
  },
  "Frost Foxfire Jar": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Blue-white foxfire shed from Still Frontier branches during a violet thaw and sealed inside a frostwood-lidded jar. It radiates clear cold light, refuses ordinary heat, and keeps delicate reagents cool for a season; opening the jar releases it as a slow spiral that settles on the nearest frostpine rather than burning anything. Harvesters collect only motes already leaving the tree. Price: 50-90 cp per sealed jar.",
    "name": "Frost Foxfire Jar"
  },
  "Preservation Moss": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Velvet-grey living moss lifted in intact sheets from Dim March nursery beds. Wrapped around food, herbs, monster materials, or fragile reagents, it slows ordinary decay while allowing dangerous magical pressure to bleed away into harmless pale specks. The moss must be aired and returned to damp soil between uses or it becomes inert rather than infinitely reusable. Price: 15-40 cp per bundle.",
    "name": "Preservation Moss"
  },
  "Muted Spirit-Stone": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A river-smooth nodule grown where Dim March moss compacts spent magical vibration into mineral. It absorbs weak sound and enchantment tremor, warming by a fraction as it fills; left overnight in living stillmoss, it empties safely. Builders set small pieces into quiet rooms and containment boxes, but a single stone cannot silence combat, speech, or powerful magic. Price: 40-80 cp.",
    "name": "Muted Spirit-Stone"
  },
  "Silence-Thread Fungus": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A pale filament fungus found as webbing beneath deep Dim March moss, each strand faintly iridescent when held against foxfire. Spun into cord or cloth, it dampens rustle, scrape, and transmitted vibration without erasing sound entirely. The fibers lose their effect if scorched and regain flexibility after a night in cool mist. Price: 25-60 cp per bundle.",
    "name": "Silence-Thread Fungus"
  },
  "Anchor-Stone": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Dense grey stone grown along the Grey Boundary's buried braces, flecked with wine-red mineral where anchor beetles have worked it. It resists unwanted magical displacement and lends stability to portals, wards, foundations, and long-lived enchantments; worked pieces become heavier when someone attempts to teleport them. Only naturally loosened blocks are safe to quarry without weakening the moor. Price: 1-3 gp.",
    "name": "Anchor-Stone"
  },
  "Fog Crystal": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A translucent crystal formed when Grey Boundary fog freezes around a thread of boundary moss. Mana drawn across its surface releases cold, opaque mist in proportion to the energy supplied; when the flow ends, the mist returns to the crystal as clear droplets. It is useful for concealment and humidity control, not for creating endless fog. Price: 5-12 sp.",
    "name": "Fog Crystal"
  },
  "Boundary Moss": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Silver-grey moss whose curled tips all lean toward the nearest active spatial distortion. A transplanted patch remains responsive for several weeks if kept damp with native fog, making it a practical but imprecise planar alarm. It indicates direction and strain only—it cannot identify a traveler, destination, or cause. Price: 30-70 cp.",
    "name": "Boundary Moss"
  },
  "Condensed Dimensional Mist": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Raw Grey Boundary vapor sealed inside a reinforced crystal ampoule, swirling around an empty center that never touches the glass. A measured drop lets spatial alchemists soften a seam, test a portal lattice, or make a small distance briefly elastic; careless release causes nearby steps and reaches to disagree until the mist disperses. It never opens the Demi-Plane without its established Gate or Anchor. Price: 8-20 sp.",
    "name": "Condensed Dimensional Mist"
  },
  "Horizon Grass": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Long gold-green blades from the Open Horizon whose tips bend toward the clearest unobstructed route. Dried into a fan or bundle, they continue indicating passable open ground until they crumble, making them valuable to navigators in fog, tall grass, and ruins. They do not locate a person, secret, or destination—only the least obstructed direction. Price: 10-25 cp.",
    "name": "Horizon Grass"
  },
  "Windseed": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A pearl-light seed bearing a translucent spiral vane. Even a breath can carry it across a room, while deliberate mana sends a whole pouch riding one chosen gust before the seeds root or lose their glow. Alchemists use ground vane-shell in mobility draughts; farmers use living seed to reveal prevailing currents before planting. Price: 20-50 cp per pouch.",
    "name": "Windseed"
  },
  "Ambient Mana Crystal": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A naturally grown crystal containing diffuse ecological mana in slow-moving bands rather than a potion's concentrated restorative charge. It powers small lamps, tools, wards, and workshop devices steadily, then becomes clear inert quartz until left in a healthy mana current to recharge. Drawing it dry too quickly cracks its inner bands and ruins the cycle. Price: 1-3 sp.",
    "name": "Ambient Mana Crystal"
  },
  "Fadeglass": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A thin plate formed where two versions of a Fade hillside briefly overlap and solidify. Its center remains visible while its rim disappears when viewed indirectly, making it superb for subtle inlay, illusion frames, and concealed but physical seams. Looking through it blurs edges and distance; it does not reveal invisible people or hidden truths. Price: 5-12 sp.",
    "name": "Fadeglass"
  },
  "Mist Pearl": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A cool opalescent pearl grown where boundary mist repeatedly condenses around one transparent seed husk. It is always faintly damp and releases a palm-sized halo of pearly vapor when warmed, then slowly reabsorbs it. Water, illusion, and spatial alchemists prize the pearl's stable rhythm. Price: 3-8 sp.",
    "name": "Mist Pearl"
  },
  "Soundless Stone": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Charcoal-grey stone from the solid side of the Fade, veined with transparent mineral. It produces no audible noise when struck, dropped, cut, or broken because vibration passes into the clear veins and leaves as a brief chill. It is used for precision tools and quiet construction, but it does not silence whatever touches or stands near it. Price: 4-10 sp.",
    "name": "Soundless Stone"
  },
  "Nullstone": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Rare matte stone shed in thin slabs near the Last Edge, where weak enchantments gutter against its surface like candlelight in rain. It suppresses minor magic in direct contact and helps line containment tools; stronger effects overwhelm it rather than vanishing. Living anchor lichen marks pieces safe to collect, preventing careless quarrying of the fixed coordinate. Price: 1-3 gp.",
    "name": "Nullstone"
  },
  "Fixed-Point Fragment": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A needle of grey-violet crystal naturally shed when the Last Edge relieves spatial stress. Once lifted, it remains metaphysically tied to the exact formation point: teleportation leaves the fragment behind, and attempts to move it extradimensionally make it impossibly heavy. Planar artisans use that refusal as a reference when calibrating stable gates, never as a substitute for the Demi-Plane Anchor. Price: 2-5 gp.",
    "name": "Fixed-Point Fragment"
  },
  "Glow-Reed": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A hollow Low Reaches reed with pale green-blue veins that fill with stored marsh light after sunset. Dried stalks remain flexible and glow when damp, lending themselves to lantern screens, baskets, breathing tubes, and alchemical wickwork. Cutting above the lowest joint lets the living root send up two new stems. Price: 10-25 cp per bundle.",
    "name": "Glow-Reed"
  },
  "Spirit Peat": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Black-blue peat formed from old glow-reed, waterplant, and moss after the moving marsh completes a resting cycle. It burns with a clean turquoise flame hot enough for alchemical furnaces yet leaves mineral-rich ash that stabilizes spirit mixtures. Fresh peat must cure through one pool migration or its trapped mana spits unpredictably. Price: 15-35 cp per brick.",
    "name": "Spirit Peat"
  },
  "Luminous Waterplant": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A soft aquatic plant with branching blue veins and clear leaves that fold around impurities. Placed in clean water, it absorbs weak toxins and excess ambient mana, brightening as it fills; a saturated plant must be composted in living marsh rather than eaten. Healers use carefully rinsed young leaves in salves and water-breathing preparations. Price: 20-50 cp.",
    "name": "Luminous Waterplant"
  },
  "Spirit Clay": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Pearl-grey clay from Hollow Vale terrace banks, threaded with foxfire moss fibers finer than hair. It accepts runes, fingerprints, and brushwork with exceptional fidelity before firing, then develops a warm inner sheen where intent was repeated through actual craft. It is favored for talisman tablets and ritual vessels but does not enchant itself without skill and materials. Price: 25-60 cp per block.",
    "name": "Spirit Clay"
  },
  "Foxfire Moss": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Soft sanctuary-and-vale moss bearing pinpricks of blue-violet foxfire between its leaves. It cushions impact, marks healthy root flow, and releases a clean cedar fragrance when disturbed; transplanted mats provide gentle light and help spirit-sensitive plants establish. Stripped of living substrate, it dims within days. Price: 20-50 cp per bundle.",
    "name": "Foxfire Moss"
  },
  "Mana Amber": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Golden resin crystallized around ancient-looking prairie roots when the Deep Expanse stores more ambient mana than living tissue can safely hold. Inside each piece, a slow speck of light follows the original grain. Cut amber makes excellent enchantment cores and jewelry, while raw nodules can be replanted beside exhausted soil to release their charge gradually. Price: 3-8 sp.",
    "name": "Mana Amber"
  },
  "Warmroot": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Consumable",
    "description": "A rose-gold Southroll rhizome that stays pleasantly warm after harvest and smells of ginger, honey, and sun-dried grass. Roasted or brewed, it eases ordinary cold and fatigue and helps the body recover warmth after exposure without healing wounds by itself. Hill foxes replant overlooked pieces, so responsible harvesters deliberately leave the smallest roots behind. Price: 15-40 cp.",
    "name": "Warmroot"
  },
  "Firefly Dust": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Gold dust naturally shed when Southroll fireflies complete one of their disciplined evening spirals. Each mote rises toward warmth and settles on moving air, giving ink, cosmetics, cloth, and festival lanterns a living shimmer that lasts several nights. Dust shaken from a captured firefly is dull; only freely shed material retains the pattern. Price: 25-60 cp per vial.",
    "name": "Firefly Dust"
  },
  "Rift Crystal": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A black-blue crystal threaded with cold cobalt light, grown where stone pressure and unstable spatial mana meet safely in Rift March clefts. It stores earth or spatial energy densely but broadcasts strain as a delayed ringing through nearby rock. Master artisans value it for powerful devices; novices learn quickly why every piece needs a stable darkstone housing. Price: 5-15 sp.",
    "name": "Rift Crystal"
  },
  "Darkstone": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Dense near-black Rift March rock whose fine grain drinks heat and resists fracture. Cold-light fungus traces natural fault lines across exposed blocks, showing where a clean quarry cut can be made without opening deeper clefts. Once worked, the stone holds fortifications and heavy enchantments with unshowy permanence. Price: 40-90 cp per workable block.",
    "name": "Darkstone"
  },
  "Blue Mana Ore": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Metal-bearing ore crossed by vivid blue veins that brighten under hammer blows. Properly smelted with vent heat and darkstone flux, it yields a conductive alloy that carries mana without the brittleness of raw crystal; overheated ore vents its charge as harmless blue sparks but loses value. Echo bats often roost near the richest safe seams. Price: 2-6 sp per chunk.",
    "name": "Blue Mana Ore"
  },
  "Deep Fungus": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "A broad, dark cave fungus with cobalt gills, nourished by mineral seepage and the Rift March's cold light. It draws unstable trace mana from stone into chewy, bitter flesh that becomes safe once dried; alchemists use it in defensive tonics and mineral-restorative salves. A pale gill edge means the colony is still filtering and should not be harvested. Price: 20-50 cp.",
    "name": "Deep Fungus"
  },
  "Moon-Sand": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
    "description": "Fine Quiet Shore sand that looks white by day and holds blue-violet constellations beneath moonlight or foxfire. Heated into glass, those points keep their arrangement, allowing artisans to make divination bowls, lunar lenses, and windows that show true reflected light without revealing anything outside their view. Loose sand flows uphill toward the not-water during false midnight. Price: 30-70 cp per pouch.",
    "name": "Moon-Sand"
  },
  "Shell Crystal": {
    "bonuses": [],
    "category": "Material",
